  {
    "episode_id": 91649,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91649.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 40,
    "turn_text": "Well, I'm fortunate to have had many mentors and sources of inspiration, but I would have to put my parents at the very top. Both of them were very involved in public service. Each of them served as mayor of my hometown of Caribou. My father, my grandfather, my great-grandfather all served in the main legislature. My mother was the first woman to chair the University of Maine System Board of Trustees. She was also head of Catholic Charities of Maine's board. I always said that she always became the chairman of any board that she was either elected or appointed to, including being chair of the school board in Caribou when I graduated. So I opened my diploma and she had signed it along with the superintendent of schools. So that was a special moment. So certainly they were my top two role models. There have been others throughout history. Margaret J. Smith was someone that I met when I was a senior in high school. And I remember by her example, she taught me that women could do anything. She was the only woman in the Senate at the time. Bill Cohen, for whom I worked for nearly 12 years when he was a member of the House and a member of the Senate, was also an inspiring role model for me. And going back further in history, Joshua Chamberlain, who could forget him leading our troops in Gettysburg. We're fortunate in main history that we have had a lot of great role models.",
